+++
title: Assyria
description: Major Mesopotamian civilization
hook: Per estimates by Karen Radner, an official message could cross the 700
  kilometers (430 miles) from Assyria's western border province of Quwê to its
  heartland in less than five days, over lands with many rivers and no
  bridges. That speed was not surpassed in the Middle East until the Ottoman
  Empire introduced the telegraph in 1865.
standfirst: "A Mesopotamian civilization that existed from the 21st to 7th
  centuries BC, conquering and administering most of West Asia from its
  heartland in Upper Mesopotamia: early Bronze Age to late Iron Age. The
  Assyrian people survive to this day, in that ancient heartland and in the
  diaspora."
key-facts:
  - name: The first capital
    value: Assur, founded c. 2600 BC and an independent city-state from c. 2025 BC
  - name: Where the throne sat
    value: Assur (c. 2025–1233 BC), Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta (c. 1233–1207 BC), Assur
      again (c. 1207–879 BC), Nimrud (879–706 BC), Dur-Sharrukin (706–705 BC),
      Nineveh (705–612 BC), Harran (612–609 BC)
  - name: The line of kings
    value: From Puzur-Ashur I, c. 2025 BC, to Ashur-uballit II, the last, 612–609 BC
  - name: The periods
    value: Old Assyrian c. 2025–1364 BC, Middle Assyrian c. 1363–912 BC,
      Neo-Assyrian 911–609 BC, from the Bronze Age to the Iron Age
  - name: Language, religion, government
    value: Akkadian, Sumerian and Aramaic; Ancient Mesopotamian religion; monarchy
  - name: How it ended
    value: Conquest by the Neo-Babylonian and Median empires in 609 BC; the sack
      and destruction of Assur by the Sasanian Empire c. AD 240
pull-quotes:
  - text: Subjects of (all) four (parts of the world), of foreign tongues, with
      different languages without similarity, people from mountainous regions
      and plains, so many (different people) as the light of the gods, lord
      above all, supervises, I let dwell inside [my new city] on the command
      of Ashur my lord [\...]. Born Assyrians, experienced in all professions,
      I set above them as supervisors and guides to teach them how to work
      properly and respect the gods and the king.
    attribution: Sargon II, from his account of the construction of Dur-Sharrukin
    section: personal-identity-and-modern-continuity
  - text: broaden the land of Ashur
    attribution: Ashur, in texts describing the coronation of Middle and
      Neo-Assyrian kings
    section: kingship
  - text: filthy //ʾāthor//, mother of corruption
    attribution: Ephrem the Syrian, 4th-century Syriac writer, on the Sasanian Empire
    section: personal-identity-and-modern-continuity
timeline:
  - when: c. 2600 BC
    what: Assur is founded.
    from: "foundation-of-assur: c. 2600 BC"
  - when: 24th century BC
    what: The name "Assur" is first attested for the site, in documents of the
      Akkadian period.
    from: The name "Assur" is first attested for the site in documents of the [[
      Akkadian period ]] in the 24th century BC.[[ ^31 ]]
  - when: c. 2025 BC
    what: The Third Dynasty of Ur collapses and Assur becomes an independent
      city-state under Puzur-Ashur I.
    from: In c. 2025 BC, due to the collapse of the Third Dynasty of Ur, Assur
      became an independent [[ city-state ]] under [[ Puzur-Ashur I ]].[[ ^34
      ]]
  - when: c. 1808 BC
    what: Shamshi-Adad I, the Amorite ruler of Ekallatum, conquers Assur, ending
      the original city-state and the Puzur-Ashur dynasty.
    from: The original Assur city-state, and the Puzur-Ashur dynasty, came to an
      end c. 1808 BC when the city was conquered by the [[ Amorite | amorites
      ]] ruler of [[ Ekallatum ]], [[ Shamshi-Adad I ]].[[ ^41 ]]
  - when: c. 1700 BC
    what: Bel-bani becomes king, ending the infighting at Assur, and founds the
      Adaside dynasty, which ruled Assyria for about a thousand years.
    from: The infighting came to an end after the rise of [[ Bel-bani ]] as king
      c. 1700 BC.[[ ^49 ]][[ ^50 ]] Bel-bani founded the [[ Adaside dynasty
      ]], which after his reign ruled Assyria for about a thousand years.[[
      ^51 ]]
  - when: c. 1430 BC
    what: Assur is subjugated by Mitanni, an arrangement that lasted for about 70
      years, until c. 1360 BC.
    from: Around c. 1430 BC, Assur was subjugated by Mitanni, an arrangement that
      lasted for about 70 years, until c. 1360 BC.[[ ^53 ]]
  - when: c. 1363 BC
    what: Ashur-uballit I wins independence again; his rise traditionally marks
      the beginning of the Middle Assyrian Empire.
    from: After his invasion, Assyria succeeded in freeing itself from its
      suzerain, achieving independence once more under [[ Ashur-uballit I ]]
      (r. c. 1363–1328 BC) whose rise to power, independence, and conquests of
      neighboring territory traditionally marks the rise of the Middle
      Assyrian Empire (c. 1363–912 BC).[[ ^54 ]]
  - when: c. 1233 BC
    what: Tukulti-Ninurta I inaugurates Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta as capital — the first
      transfer of administrative power away from Assur.
    from: The first transfer of administrative power away from Assur occurred
      under Tukulti-Ninurta I,[[ ^60 ]] who c. 1233 BC[[ ^61 ]] inaugurated
      Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta as capital.[[ ^60 ]]
  - when: c. 1207 BC
    what: Tukulti-Ninurta I is assassinated; inter-dynastic conflict and a
      significant drop in Assyrian power follow.
    from: Tukulti-Ninurta I's assassination c. 1207 BC was followed by
      inter-dynastic conflict and a significant drop in Assyrian power.[[ ^62
      ]]
  - when: 911 BC
    what: The end of Ashur-dan II's reign conventionally marks the beginning of
      the Neo-Assyrian Empire (911–609 BC).
    from: Under [[ Ashur-dan II ]] (r. 934–912 BC), who campaigned in the
      northeast and northwest, Assyrian decline was at last reversed, paving
      the way for grander efforts under his successors. The end of his reign
      conventionally marks the beginning of the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]]
      (911–609 BC).[[ ^67 ]]
  - when: 879 BC
    what: Ashurnasirpal II restores the ancient and ruined town of Nimrud and
      designates it the new capital of the empire.
    from: Ashurnasirpal restored the ancient and ruined town of [[ Nimrud ]], also
      located in the Assyrian heartland, and in 879 BC designated that city as
      the new capital of the empire.[[ ^71 ]]
  - when: 729 BC
    what: Tiglath-Pileser III conquers Babylonia.
    from: The most significant conquests were the vassalization of the [[ Levant
      ]] all the way to the Egyptian border and the 729 BC conquest of [[
      Babylonia ]].[[ ^76 ]]
  - when: 706 BC
    what: Sargon II moves the capital to Dur-Sharrukin, a city he built himself.
    from: In 706 BC, Sargon II transferred the capital to the city of
      Dur-Sharrukin, which he built himself.[[ ^78 ]]
  - when: 705 BC
    what: Sargon II dies and Sennacherib immediately transfers the capital to
      Nineveh.
    from: Immediately after Sargon II's death in 705 BC, his son Sennacherib
      transferred the capital to Nineveh,[[ ^79 ]][[ ^132 ]] a far more
      natural seat of power.[[ ^132 ]]
  - when: 671 BC
    what: The conquest of Egypt under Esarhaddon brings Assyria to its greatest
      ever extent.
    from: The 671 BC [[ conquest of Egypt | assyrian-conquest-of-egypt ]] under [[
      Esarhaddon ]] (r. 681–669 BC) brought Assyria to its greatest ever
      extent.[[ ^77 ]]
  - when: 626 BC
    what: Babylon revolts under Nabopolassar.
    from: The revolt of Babylon under [[ Nabopolassar ]] in 626 BC, in combination
      with an invasion by the [[ Medes ]] under [[ Cyaxares ]] in 615/614 BC,
      led to the [[ Medo-Babylonian conquest of the Assyrian Empire ]].[[ ^81
      ]]
  - when: 614 BC
    what: Assur is sacked.
    from: "[[ Assur was sacked | fall-of-assur ]] in 614 BC and [[ Nineveh fell |
      fall-of-nineveh ]] in 612 BC.[[ ^82 ]]"
  - when: 612 BC
    what: Nineveh falls.
    from: "[[ Assur was sacked | fall-of-assur ]] in 614 BC and [[ Nineveh fell |
      fall-of-nineveh ]] in 612 BC.[[ ^82 ]]"
  - when: 609 BC
    what: Ashur-uballit II, the last Assyrian ruler, is defeated at Harran — the
      end of the ancient line of Assyrian kings and of Assyria as a state.
    from: The last Assyrian ruler, [[ Ashur-uballit II ]], tried to rally the
      Assyrian army at [[ Harran ]] in the west but he was defeated in 609 BC,
      marking the end of the ancient line of Assyrian kings and of Assyria as
      a state.[[ ^83 ]][[ ^84 ]]
  - when: Toward the end of the 6th century BC
    what: The Assyrian dialect of the Akkadian language goes extinct, Aramaic
      having already largely replaced it as a vernacular.
    from: Toward the end of the 6th century BC, the Assyrian dialect of the [[
      Akkadian language ]] went extinct, having toward the end of the
      Neo-Assyrian Empire already largely been replaced by Aramaic as a [[
      vernacular ]] language.[[ ^88 ]]
  - when: c. AD 240
    what: The Sasanian Empire sacks Assur; the Ashur temple is destroyed and the
      city's population dispersed.
    from: This last cultural golden age came to an end with the sack of Assur by
      the [[ Sasanian Empire ]] c. 240.[[ ^99 ]] During the sack, the Ashur
      temple was destroyed again and the city's population was dispersed.[[
      ^97 ]]
  - when: 14th century
    what: Suppression and massacres under the Ilkhanate and the Timurid Empire
      relegate the Assyrians to a local ethnic and religious minority.
    from: Despite the loss of political power, the Assyrians continued to
      constitute a significant portion of the population in northern
      Mesopotamia until religiously motivated suppression and massacres under
      the [[ Ilkhanate ]] and the [[ Timurid Empire ]] in the 14th century,
      which relegated them to a local ethnic and religious minority.[[ ^101 ]]
image-plan:
  - file: Iraq;_Nimrud_-_Assyria,_Lamassu's_Guarding_Palace_Entrance.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: One of the entrances of the Northwest Palace at Nimrud, the Assyrian
      capital 879–706 BC, destroyed by the Islamic State in 2015.
  - file: Female_head_from_Assur,_Iraq,_2400-2100_BCE._Pergamon_Museum.jpg
    role: full
    section: early-history
    caption: The head of a female statue from Assur, dating back to the Akkadian
      period (c. 2334–2154 BC). Her eyes, eyebrows and elaborate hair covering
      were originally inlaid. Pergamon Museum, Berlin.
  - file: KültepeUnterstadt1.jpg
    role: inline
    section: early-history
    caption: The ruins of the Old Assyrian trading colony at Kültepe, where 22,000
      clay tablets from Assur's trade network were found.
  - file: Tilglath_pileser_iii.jpg
    role: inline
    section: assyrian-empire
    caption: A partial relief of Tiglath-Pileser III, r. 745–727 BC, under whom
      the Neo-Assyrian Empire was consolidated, centralized and significantly
      expanded.
  - file: Detail._Parthian_stele_from_Ashur,_Iraq._1st_century_BCE_to_1st_century_CE._Museum_of_Archaeology,_Istanbul,_Turkey.jpg
    role: inline
    section: later-history
    caption: Detail of a stele in the style of the Neo-Assyrian royal steles,
      erected in Assur in the 2nd century AD under Parthian rule by the local
      ruler Rʻuth-Assor — long after the empire had fallen.
  - file: Erishum_I_of_Assyria.png
    role: inline
    section: kingship
    caption: A line-drawing of a royal seal of the Old Assyrian king Erishum I, r.
      c. 1974–1934 BC. The seated ruler is thought to represent the god Ashur,
      with Erishum the bald figure being led toward him.
  - file: Alabaster_Stela_of_the_Asirian_King_Ashurnasirpal_II_(884-859_BC)_-_British_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: kingship
    caption: A stele of the Neo-Assyrian king Ashurnasirpal II, r. 883–859 BC,
      under whom Assyria became the dominant political power in the Near East.
  - file: Stele_of_Bel-harran-beli-usur,_from_Tell_Abda,_8th_century_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum,_Istanbul,_Turkey.jpg
    role: inline
    section: aristocracy-and-elite
    caption: Stele of Bel-harran-beli-usur, a palace herald — one of the offices
      held by the magnates — made in the reign of Shalmaneser IV (r. 783–773
      BC).
  - file: Stele_of_Ili-ittija_governor_of_Libbi-ali,_Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta,_Ekallatum,_Itu,_and_Ruqahu._From_Assur,_Iraq._804_BCE._Pergamon_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: administration
    caption: A stele of Ili-ittija, governor of Libbi-ali, Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta,
      Ekallatum, Itu, and Ruqahu, c. 804 BC. Governors handled local order,
      public safety and economy.
  - file: Assyrian_spearman_·_HHWI469.svg
    role: inline
    section: military
    caption: A 20th-century illustration of a Neo-Assyrian spearman.
  - file: Denis_Bourez_-_British_Museum,_London_(8748213226).jpg
    role: full
    section: populace
    caption: A Neo-Assyrian relief of Assyrians in a procession. Most Assyrians
      were farmers working land owned by their families.
  - file: Naqi'a_crop.png
    role: inline
    section: status-of-women
    caption: Naqi'a, Sennacherib's wife, 8th–7th centuries BC — the most
      documented woman in Assyrian history, who influenced politics under
      three kings.
  - file: Cuneiform_tablet_case_impressed_with_four_cylinder_seals_Old_Assyrian.png
    role: inline
    section: economy
    caption: An Old Assyrian tablet from Kültepe recording the repayment of a
      loan, impressed with four different cylinder seals.
  - file: Ashurbanipal_wall_relief,_7th_century_BC,_from_Nineveh,_the_British_Museum.jpg
    role: full
    section: personal-identity-and-modern-continuity
    caption: A 7th-century BC relief from Nineveh depicting Ashurbanipal (r.
      669–631 BC) and two royal attendants.
  - file: Cuneiform_tablet-_caravan_account_MET_DP-13441-005.jpg
    role: inline
    section: akkadian
    caption: An Old Assyrian cuneiform tablet containing an account of a caravan
      journey.
  - file: Syriac_papyri.jpg
    role: inline
    section: aramaic-and-other-languages
    caption: A 9th-century AD piece of papyrus with Syriac writing — the language
      that displaced Akkadian in Mesopotamia.
  - file: Artist’s_impression_of_Assyrian_palaces_from_The_Monuments_of_Nineveh_by_Sir_Austen_Henry_Layard,_1853.jpg
    role: full
    section: architecture
    caption: A 19th-century reconstruction of Nineveh, the Assyrian capital
      705–612 BC.
  - file: The_Monuments_of_Nineveh_(BM_1849,0519.1-102)_(cropped).jpg
    role: inline
    section: art
    caption: Austen Henry Layard's illustration "The Monuments of Nineveh", 1849.
  - file: Ancient_Egyptian,_Assyrian,_and_Persian_costumes_and_decorations_(1920)_(14741970056).jpg
    role: inline
    section: art
    caption: A 20th-century illustration of decorative patterns found in ancient
      Assyrian reliefs and garments.
  - file: Library_of_Ashurbanipal_The_Flood_Tablet.jpg
    role: inline
    section: scholarship-and-literature
    caption: A tablet from the Library of Ashurbanipal containing a portion of the
      Epic of Gilgamesh. The library held more than 30,000 documents.
  - file: Nestorian_archbishop_and_servants.jpg
    role: inline
    section: christianity
    caption: An early 20th-century archbishop of the Assyrian Church of the East,
      with entourage.
glossary:
  - term: //māt Aššur//
    gloss: '"Land of Ashur": what official documents called Assyria from its rise
      as a territorial state in the 14th century BC onward, marking its shift
      to being a regional polity.'
    from: From the time of its rise as a territorial state in the 14th century BC
      and onward, Assyria was referred to in official documents as //māt
      Aššur// ("land of Ashur"), marking its shift to being a regional polity.
  - term: //iššiak Aššur//
    gloss: "\"Governor [on behalf] of Ashur\" — the style of the Old Assyrian
      kings, Ashur himself being considered the city's formal king."
    from: The Old Assyrian kings were styled as //iššiak Aššur// ("governor [on
      behalf] of [[ Ashur | ashur-god ]]"), with Ashur being considered the
      city's formal king.[[ ^113 ]]
  - term: //limmu//
    gloss: A yearly office-holder elected from the influential men of Assyria, who
      gave their name to the year, so that their name appeared in all
      administrative documents signed that year.
    from: a yearly office-holder, a //[[ limmu ]]// official, was elected from the
      influential men of Assyria. The //limmu// official gave their name to
      the year, meaning that their name appeared in all administrative
      documents signed that year.
  - term: The magnates
    gloss: The set of high-ranking Neo-Assyrian offices — treasurer, palace
      herald, chief cupbearer, chief officer/eunuch, chief judge, grand vizier
      and commander-in-chief.
    from: The Neo-Assyrian inner elite is typically divided by modern scholars
      into the "magnates", a set of high-ranking offices, and the "scholars"
      (//ummânī//), tasked with advising and guiding the kings through
      interpreting omens. The magnates included the offices //masennu//
      (treasurer), //nāgir ekalli// (palace herald), //[[ rab šāqê | rabshakeh
      ]]// (chief cupbearer), //rab ša-rēši// (chief officer/eunuch),
      //sartinnu// (chief judge), //sukkallu// (grand vizier) and //[[ turtanu
      ]]// (commander-in-chief), which at times continued to be occupied by
      royal family members.[[ ^138 ]]
  - term: //pāḫutu//
    gloss: The provinces or districts Assyrian territory was divided into from the
      beginning of the Middle Assyrian period, each headed by a governor.
    from: Assyrian territory was divided into a set of provinces or districts
      (//pāḫutu//).[[ ^144 ]] The total number and size of these provinces
      varied and changed as Assyria expanded and contracted.[[ ^145 ]] Every
      province was headed by a provincial governor
  - term: //ḫarımtū//
    gloss: Women historically believed to have been prostitutes, but today
      interpreted as women with an independent social existence — not tied to
      a husband, father, or institution.
    from: The //ḫarımtū// women have historically been believed to have been
      prostitutes, but today, are interpreted as women with an independent
      social existence, i.e. not tied to a husband, father, or institution.
  - term: //lamassu//
    gloss: Colossi, often human-headed lions or bulls, placed at the gates of
      temples, palaces and cities.
    from: Another well-known form of Neo-Assyrian art are colossi, often
      human-headed lions or bulls (//[[ lamassu ]]//), that were placed at the
      gates of temples, palaces and cities.
  - term: Standard Babylonian
    gloss: A highly codified version of ancient Babylonian as used around 1500 BC,
      used in Assyria as a language of high culture — scholarly documents,
      literature, poetry and royal inscriptions.
    from: Standard Babylonian was a highly codified version of ancient Babylonian,
      as used around 1500 BC, and was used as a language of high culture, for
      nearly all scholarly documents, literature, poetry[[ ^218 ]][[ ^220 ]]
      and royal inscriptions.[[ ^215 ]]
further:
  - slug: neo-assyrian-empire
    why: The 911–609 BC empire that ruled the largest territory then yet
      assembled, from parts of modern-day Iran to Egypt.
  - slug: post-imperial-assyria
    why: "What happened after 609 BC: the sites resettled, the Ashur temple
      restored under the Parthians, and the final sack c. AD 240."
  - slug: hanging-gardens-of-babylon
    why: Sennacherib may even have been responsible for building them at Nineveh —
      one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.
anchors:
  hook: Per estimates by [[ Karen Radner ]], an official message sent in the
    Neo-Assyrian period from the western border province [[ Quwê ]] to the
    Assyrian heartland, a distance of 700 kilometers (430 miles) over a
    stretch of lands featuring many rivers without any bridges, could take
    less than five days to arrive. Such communication speed was unprecedented
    before the rise of the Neo-Assyrian Empire and was not surpassed in the
    Middle East until the [[ telegraph ]] was introduced by the Ottoman Empire
    in 1865, nearly two and a half thousand years after the Neo-Assyrian
    Empire's fall.[[ ^154 ]][[ ^153 ]]
  standfirst: "!!Assyria!![[ ^e ]] was a major ancient [[ Mesopotamian |
    mesopotamia ]] civilization that existed from the 21st to 7th centuries BC
    which conquered and administered most of [[ West Asia ]] from the [[
    Assyrian heartland | assyrian-homeland ]] in [[ Upper Mesopotamia ]].[[ ^4
    ]] Assyria is called the first true [[ military superpower | superpower ]]
    in human history, and pioneered major inventions and advancements in the
    first [[ libraries | library ]] and large-scale preservation and
    accumulation of [[ knowledge ]], [[ statecraft ]], [[ centralised
    government | centralized-government ]], [[ bureaucracy ]], [[ warfare |
    war ]], military organisation, [[ propaganda ]], [[ art ]], and [[
    architecture ]]. Assyrian imperial history spans the early [[ Bronze Age
    ]] to the late [[ Iron Age ]], from its beginning as small [[ Akkadian |
    akkadian-empire ]] settlements in northern Mesopotamia to its zenith and
    collapse as the largest empire to date at the time, and is typically
    divided into the [[ Early Assyrian | early-assyrian-period ]] (c.
    2600–2025 BC), [[ Old Assyrian | old-assyrian-period ]] (c. 2025–1364 BC),
    [[ Middle Assyrian | middle-assyrian-empire ]] (c. 1363–912 BC), [[
    Neo-Assyrian | neo-assyrian-empire ]] (911–609 BC), and [[ post-imperial |
    post-imperial-assyria ]] (609 BC–c. 240 AD) periods.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]] The
    [[ Assyrian people | assyrians ]] survive to this day and continue to live
    and practice their culture in their ancient heartland and in the
    diaspora."
image: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/Ashur_symbol_Nimrud.png
